bold>, apical and <bold>B</bold>, equatorial shortaxis views during stress; <bold>C</bold> and <bold>D</bold> show perfusion images at rest. <italic>E,</italic> Coronary angiography shows 90% stenosis (white arrow) of proximal LAD. With permission of [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="R2">2</xref>].</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CCR-5-237_F2"/></fig><fig id="F3" position="float"><label>Fig. (3)</label><caption><p>Late gadolinium enhancement images of a patient with chronic myocardial infarction of the septal ventricular wall, including the apex (white arrows). Images in basal short-axis view <bold>(A)</bold> and 4-chamber view <bold>(B)</bold> at 3 T acquired 15 minutes after injection of 0.15 mmol gadolinium/kg body weight.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CCR-5-237_F3"/></fig><fig id="F4" position="float"><label>Fig. (4)</label><caption><p>                        <bold>(A)</bold> Black-blood image at 3 T of the proximal left anterior descending (LAD) (white arrow) in a CAD patient shows thickened coronary vessel wall (zoomed image &#x02013; white arrow).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CCR-5-237_F4"/></fig></floats-group></article>